# Private config source pointer (git-ignored) — a consumer-side convention.
#
# The framework ships only committed templates for the operational data
# (etc/machines.ini, etc/reuter.ini, etc/team.ini, ...). The real values are
# private and must not enter the public Git history: keep them in a separate,
# access-controlled private repository — a small git repo whose tracked files
# mirror the consumer's etc/ (machines.ini, reuter.ini, team.ini, plus any
# consumer-specific dev-only files such as `hosts`, a hostname→IP mapping).
#
# Fetching and injecting that data is a consumer concern: the framework does
# not read this file (its dev-init/deploy tooling, or plain symlinks into
# etc/, are the consumer's own business). This file documents the shape of the
# pointer so consumers share one convention — copy it to `.private-source`
# (untracked, machine-specific) and set the retrieval mechanism: a git URL
# (+ optional ref), fetched on demand into var/private-data (git-ignored, like
# the rest of /var/):
#
# PRIVATE_DATA_GIT=git@example.com:team/app-consumer-config.git
# PRIVATE_DATA_REF=main
#
# The private repo's committed content is the single source of truth: no
# machine runs on a loose, uncommitted copy. Nothing in the private repo
# reaches a prod host on its own — the consumer's own deploy tooling decides
# what a host needs (typically etc/reuter.ini, shipped by the framework's
# DEPLOY_PRIVATE_FILES; the etc/deploy.conf values travel as replayed
# environment; see doc/system/consumer-config.md).
#
# This file never discloses the private repo's identity in the public history
# (it is git-ignored). Access control on the private repo is the real security
# boundary — see doc/system/consumer-config.md.
